\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Process Reward Models.}
PRMs assign step-level quality scores to guide agent behavior \citep{xi2025agentprm,choudhury2025prm,fan2026agentrm}, with benchmarks revealing degradation on long-horizon trajectories \citep{wang2026planrewardbench} and the advantage of specialized over general-purpose PRMs \citep{li2026toolprmbench}.
\citet{le2022coderl} take a complementary value-function approach operating on program states.
Our work differs in operating at window level without outcome labels, targeting real-time monitoring ($<$5\,ms) rather than training signal generation.

\paragraph{Agent Self-Reflection.}
Self-reflection methods \citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2023tot,zhou2024lats} prompt agents to diagnose their own failures, but LLMs cannot reliably self-correct without external feedback \citep{huang2024selfcorrect,kamoi2024selfcorrection}.
We address the prior question---\emph{when} is the agent stuck---using external structural signals.

\paragraph{Weak Supervision for Sequential Data.}
Data programming \citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel} enables dataset creation through labeling functions, extended to sequential data via linked HMMs \citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, multi-resolution LFs \citep{sala2019multiresolution}, and heterogeneous aggregation \citep{lison2021skweak}.
We apply this paradigm to agent trajectories, finding---unlike \citeauthor{safranchik2020linkedhmm}'s NER results---that temporal label models do not improve over majority vote in our low-conflict 3-LF regime (\S\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).

\paragraph{Noisy Labels and Label Leakage.}
Our silver labels connect to noisy label learning \citep{han2018coteaching,li2020dividemix}, though our noise is temporally block-correlated rather than instance-level.
The structural feature--label overlap instantiates label leakage \citep{kaufman2012leakage} and Goodhart's Law \citep{manheim2018goodhart}; we mitigate this through cross-coding-agent evaluation and a pending gold set (\S\ref{sec:gold_set}).

\paragraph{Agent Monitoring and Degradation.}
\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \citet{chen2025monitoring} establish that sequence-level context is necessary for monitoring; \citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; and \citet{ma2024agentboard} introduce fine-grained progress tracking.
\citet{cemri2025multiagentfail} and \citet{zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes including cascading failures in long-horizon tasks.
We complement these with a systematic comparison demonstrating that lightweight structural features suffice for detection and transfer.

\paragraph{Cycle Detection and Process Mining.}
Cycle detectors \citep{george2025cycledetection,pipis2025reasoningloop} target structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation; \citet{liu2026trajad} train trajectory verifiers for broader anomaly detection.
Our structural features draw on process mining \citep{vanderaalst2016processmining}, and the success of hand-crafted features in cross-coding-agent transfer parallels classical findings where feature-based methods outperform learned representations for narrow detection tasks \citep{liu2008isolationforest}.

